00:00:35.027 --> 00:00:37.109
Tardigrades have been through a lot.

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:38.699
They’ve been sent to the moon.

00:00:38.699 --> 00:00:41.129
They’ve had the moisture sapped out of them.

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:43.309
At times, they’ve been in extreme heat.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:46.210
And at other times, they’ve had to contend
with extreme cold.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:51.109
And they have borne this all with as much dignity
as a creature nicknamed “moss piglet”

00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:57.319
can, not knowing that on the other side would
be a whole audience of scientists and microscopy

00:00:57.319 --> 00:01:03.929
fans waiting to hear about the latest achievement
in tardigrade survival that they’ve managed to unlock.

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:07.220
Well, today, we’ve got a new one for you.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:11.920
A harrowing journey for these tardigrades
that have taken them through, what we assume,

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.560
must be the worst thing that tardigrades have
yet been subjected to.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.223
These poor, enduring tardigrades got stuck
in postal security.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:33.165
Their journey through the macrocosmos began
in a much more idyllic place: a beach in Portugal.

00:01:33.165 --> 00:01:37.550
These are marine tardigrades, which we’ve
never been able to show on our channel before

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.262
for the very simple reason that we’d never
found them before.

00:01:41.262 --> 00:01:46.371
Sadly, there is not much information available
about marine tardigrades compared to freshwater

00:01:46.371 --> 00:01:50.656
ones, so we won’t be able to dive too deep
into their lives today.

00:01:50.656 --> 00:01:57.180
But we can watch their strange gecko-like
feet as they walk across the slide, and appreciate

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.436
that they are indeed very, very weird to look
at.

00:02:01.436 --> 00:02:05.890
And James, our master of microscopes, had
been searching for marine tardigrades in the

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.789
various sand samples he gets from the Baltic
Sea.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:13.409
But he couldn’t find any, perhaps because
the water wasn’t salty enough.

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:18.319
He’d even trawled through coral farm samples
to find them, but still no luck.

00:02:18.764 --> 00:02:24.870
But then James reached out to a marine biologist
friend in Portugal named Maria and asked if

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.970
she could send some Portugal beach samples
along.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.730
You know, the usual favors between friends.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.890
So Maria went to the beach, following James’
instructions as she dug through the sand at

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:41.686
different phases of the tide, and gathered
algae and biofilm from the rocks.

00:02:41.686 --> 00:02:46.010
Then she packed up the samples in a box,
and sent it in the mail.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.064
Within that box, not yet known to either its
sender or receiver,

00:02:50.064 --> 00:02:52.660
were these Batillipes tardigrades.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.760
Batillipes is a genus of marine tardigrades.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.083
And ironically, given how difficult they are
for James to find now, the first Batillipes

00:03:00.083 --> 00:03:05.099
tardigrades were actually found in the Baltic
Sea, and since then there have been at least

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:07.991
36 species described.

00:03:07.991 --> 00:03:13.180
We don’t know exactly what species these
Batillipes are, but they do strongly resemble

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:19.752
a species called Batillipes minius that was
discovered in northern Portugal in 2015.

00:03:19.752 --> 00:03:25.321
And what was striking to James when he first
saw them is just how small they are.

00:03:25.321 --> 00:03:30.519
He’d been expecting them to be so much larger,
more on the order of their freshwater relatives

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:35.923
like this one, which at 200x looks like a
giant compared to the Batillipes.

00:03:35.923 --> 00:03:40.769
But James didn’t know that these adorable
little tardigrades were on route to him.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:44.879
When Maria sent him those samples from the
beach, they were just hypothetical little

00:03:44.879 --> 00:03:48.590
worlds teeming with unknown and unseen life.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:55.372
And then those hypothetical little worlds
got stuck in Germany for security reasons.

00:03:55.372 --> 00:03:56.419
Why?

00:03:56.419 --> 00:03:57.449
No idea.

00:03:57.449 --> 00:04:02.297
The microcosmos often has us shrugging and
saying, “We have no idea,”

00:04:02.297 --> 00:04:03.669
to its many mysteries.

00:04:03.669 --> 00:04:09.260
And I suppose that mirrors the reality of
the macrocosmos as well.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.109
Because sometimes, you find out your package
full of beach sand is going to be stuck in

00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:17.630
a German warehouse for the weekend, and all
you can do is shrug.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.380
After someone opened up the package and checked
that everything had been declared correctly,

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.265
the package, and our tardigrades, were able
to resume their journey

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.402
and make it to James’ doorstep.

00:04:27.402 --> 00:04:33.060
But unfortunately, the lack of oxygen meant
that the algae samples weren’t able to survive

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.380
their delay in the warehouse.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:41.182
But that would never stop James from trying
to find whatever he can in his samples.

00:04:41.182 --> 00:04:45.310
So he spent 16 straight hours 
looking through them.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:50.410
And to his delight, the sand samples had a
whole herd of Batillipes wandering around

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:57.463
in them, having survived a spectacular journey
they didn’t even know they were on.

